{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"RP-02 progress: SLICES 1 AND 2 COMPLETE AND LANDED ON MAIN (6 of 9 tasks, 2 of 3 stories). S3 convergent-pinelike-lunamoth remains and is now unblocked. Full trail and an exact resume point are in session log maxixe-ironic-kiskadee (d95a-aa80-dfe0-4336).\n\nLANDED, each with LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1, submitted individually per the AGENTS.md no-feature-branch fallback: 4758d47f82 (S1T1 repository) / 1429cb8d4b (S1T2 facade delegation) / 0c318685c8 (S1T3 ADR 0099 + publication guards) / 9371328844 (S2T1 identity transitions) / 250cac4d8c (S2T2 absence, tombstone, comment) / 9c3db3ea12 (S2T3 narrow rich-emission seam).\n\nOUTCOME SO FAR: binding_store.py 799 -> 561 LOC, off the cap it was pinned against. Three internal owners exist behind one unchanged public facade: binding_repository.py 360, binding_lifecycle.py 533. ADR 0099 records the design. ~130 new tests.\n\nTHE EPIC'S MOST TRANSFERABLE FINDING: four DOCUMENTED behaviours turned out never to have been implemented, each caught by writing the test the docstring implied and watching it fail against faithful code. (1) 'safety-abort on ambiguous double bind' - bind_confirm is last-writer-wins with no abort anywhere in the three modules; this one was asserted by S2's plan AND its parent story AC, so both were corrected and the term reserved for S3, where the abort is genuinely new. (2) Lifecycle alerts are documented as deduped, but is_deduped compares a timestamp_ns that no caller stamps, so suppression can never fire. (3) The comment map's 'a key is never remapped to a different id' - the change-gate is equality-only, so a differing id overwrites. (4) The rich-emission op was credited with save/dirty-gating behaviour, but the production caller never saves per emit and save() is explicitly unconditional. In every case I pinned the REAL behaviour and corrected the false statement rather than changing behaviour inside an extraction slice, and filed the ones that are genuine defects.\n\nMUTATION ALSO CLOSED THREE ORACLE GAPS my tests would otherwise have shipped: a facade copying the repository document (invisible until a NEW top-level key diverges, which would have silently lost comment identity and re-posted every mirrored comment); a rebind fixture that could not distinguish a targeted reverse pop from a sweep; and a save spy watching only the facade while the policy owner holds the repository directly.\n\nHELD-OUT TDD held throughout: implementers saw only happy-path subsets, and every withheld discriminator passed unseen (15/15, 4/4, 11/11, 13/13, 5/5 across the five code slices). Every CI failure was root-caused, never retried - a stale generated env-vars doc twice, a comment-hygiene gate rejecting a commit SHA in a source comment, and one known tracked macOS flake recheck'd with reasoning per CONTRIBUTING.md section 6.\n\nNEXT: S3 is the only slice with genuinely NEW behaviour (interrupted-retirement completion), so RED-first applies properly rather than characterization. Its three tasks run c4f7 -> 90a5 -> ef69. All are UNSIGNED; set file_impact before earning each attestation. Eight verified constraints are recorded in the session log, the sharpest being that run_differs.py has only 61 lines of headroom so S3 T2's edit there is the invocation only, and that S2 T3 has now made the apply_handlers docstring claim about 'cannot carry a narrower accessor' false, so S3 T3 must update it.\n\nRESIDUAL GAPS FILED (3, all linked discovered_from): smalltime-rapid-elk, best-kingly-monkey (more urgent now that RP-04 S7.3.a is in_progress and landing in sub-parts), captious-hennish-mangabey."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787134434895306001,"uuid":"2af3c453-a8f5-4d9b-b0ea-2d2958ed3eb8"}